lesson:
So far everything has been `static`: methods that belong to the class as a whole. Real Java programs are built from **objects**: things that hold their own data and know how to do things with it.

```
public class Plot {
    private String name;          // fields: each Plot has its own
    private double kg;

    public Plot(String name) {    // constructor: runs on new Plot(...)
        this.name = name;
        this.kg = 0;
    }

    public void harvest(double more) {   // not static: works on THIS plot
        if (more <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        kg += more;
    }

    public double getKg() { return kg; }
    public String toString() { return name + ": " + kg + " kg"; }
}

Plot north = new Plot("North");
north.harvest(40);
System.out.println(north);        // calls toString(): North: 40.0 kg
```

- `private` fields mean only the class's own methods can touch them. That's **encapsulation**: `harvest` can refuse a negative amount, and nobody can set `kg` to nonsense from outside.
- `this.name` means "this object's name", which tells it apart from the parameter `name`.
- Getters (`getKg()`) give read access without write access.
- `toString()` decides how the object prints.

Two classes in one file: only one can be `public` (the one matching the file name). For exercises, keep everything in one public class with a small helper class, or give each class its own file in real projects.

example.code:
public class Example {
    static class Plot {
        private final String name;
        private double kg;

        Plot(String name) { this.name = name; }

        void harvest(double more) {
            if (more <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("must be positive");
            kg += more;
        }

        double getKg() { return kg; }

        public String toString() { return name + ": " + kg + " kg"; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Plot north = new Plot("North"), south = new Plot("South");
        north.harvest(40);
        north.harvest(5);
        south.harvest(12);
        System.out.println(north + " | " + south);
    }
}

